          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. The surrounding qualification limits its degree rather than changing the avowal into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The expression weakly invokes Cordelia's already-existing filial bond and its duties, but it does not clearly create, renew, or promise a new commitment. Cordelia is characterising the love she owes and gives as a daughter.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The focal words are a measured avowal made in answer to Lear's demand, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Cordelia explicitly describes difficulty verbalising her heart, but the resulting statement remains controlled and propositional.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"No core force beyond avowing love and weakly invoking an existing filial bond is supported. The utterance's contrast with her sisters and resistance to Lear's demanded exaggeration shape its significance but do not establish a separate speech-act mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most, tying their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. After Cordelia initially answers \\\"Nothing\\\" and Lear orders her to speak again, she gives this restrained avowal, qualifying her love by her filial bond rather than competing in unlimited praise.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing daughter-to-father bond, later specifying duties to obey, love, and honour Lear. She does not clearly undertake a new obligation in the focal expression. Lear does not accept the answer as adequate; he tells her to amend it lest she damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text explicitly identifies the work as William Shakespeare's The Tragedy of King Lear, which I also recognise.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence supports the utterance's immediate force and shows Cordelia's own assurance that her love exceeds her words. It does not independently settle every question about sincerity, later conduct, or the full history of the relationship beyond this supplied scene.\"}"
        }
      ],
